<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Telangana</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Telangana is a state of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, with its capital at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a>. The region was ruled by the Kakatiya dynasty from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warangal">Warangal</a>, then by the Bahmani and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golconda-fort">Golconda</a> sultanates and the Nizams of Hyderabad. It became part of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> in 1956 and, after a long movement for statehood, a separate state in 2014.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/telangana.jpg" alt="Telangana" width="320" height="146" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vivekanand pokala · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:A_typical_charminar_evening.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Telangana lies on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/deccan-plateau">Deccan Plateau</a>, in the central stretch of the eastern seaboard of the Indian Peninsula, and covers 112,077 square kilometres. It borders <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maharashtra">Maharashtra</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chhattisgarh">Chhattisgarh</a> to the north and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karnataka">Karnataka</a> to the south. The Godavari and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/krishna">Krishna</a> rivers drain it, along with smaller rivers such as the Bhima, the Manjira and the Musi, but most of the land is arid. The name comes from Telinga, and was used to set the predominantly Telugu region of the old Hyderabad State apart from Marathwada and Kalyana-Karnataka.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>One of the earliest known political formations here was the Aśmaka Mahajanapada, which flourished roughly between 700 and 400 BCE and was the only southern kingdom among the sixteen great Mahajanapadas. Its capital, Podana, is identified by most scholars with present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bodhan">Bodhan</a>. The region later belonged to many empires, among them the Maurya, Satavahana, Chalukya, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a>, Rashtrakuta, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/delhi-sultanate">Delhi Sultanate</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate</a> and Golconda Sultanate. The region experienced a golden age under the Kakatiya dynasty, which ruled most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/andhra-pradesh">Andhra Pradesh</a> and Telangana from 1083 to 1323; Rudrama Devi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prataparudra-kakatiya">Prataparudra II</a> were prominent rulers. It weakened after the attack of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-kafur">Malik Kafur</a> in 1309 and was dissolved when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammad-bin-tughluq">Muhammad bin Tughluq</a>'s forces defeated Prataparudra in 1323.</p><p>From the seventeenth to the nineteenth century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mughal-empire">Mughals</a> and the Nizams of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> ruled the region. In 1823 the Nizams ceded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-circars">Northern Circars</a> and the Ceded Districts to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>, which left <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad-sindh">Hyderabad</a> a landlocked princely state. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> became independent in 1947 the Nizam wished to stay independent, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/government-of-india">Government of India</a> annexed Hyderabad State on 17 September 1948 in a military operation called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/operation-polo">Operation Polo</a>. After decades of protest by the Telangana movement, the state of Telangana was created in 2014 from the Telugu regions of the former Hyderabad State.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Telangana has <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hyderabad">Hyderabad</a> as its capital. Telugu is the primary official language and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/urdu">Urdu</a> the second; about 75 per cent of people speak Telugu and 12 per cent Urdu. Before 1948 Urdu was the official language of Hyderabad State and the language of the educated elite. At the 2011 census <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hinduism">Hindus</a> formed 85.1 per cent of the population and Muslims 12.7 per cent. Warangal Fort, the Kakatiya capital, has the Kakatiya Kala Thoranam, which appears on the state emblem, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ramappa-temple">Ramappa Temple</a> is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>. The state is divided into 33 districts.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Telangana” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Tughluq armies failed to recover the region, and by his death in 1358 Bahman Shah controlled a kingdom stretching from the Arabian Sea coast to the borders of <b>Telangana</b>, bounded in the south by the Krishna and Tungabhadra rivers, where it faced the newly founded Vijayanagara Empire.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bahmani-sultanate">Bahmani Sultanate · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Great nobles, including the paigah families and other jagirdars, held vast estates, and land in the <b>Telangana</b> districts was dominated by landlords (deshmukhs) who extracted rent and forced labour (vetti) from peasants.</blockquote><a href="/empire/hyderabad-state">Hyderabad State · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>In India Mao’s ideas found followers in the <b>Telangana</b> uprising of 1946–1951, and after the Naxalbari uprising of 1967 Charu Majumdar had Quotations from Chairman Mao Zedong read aloud to illiterate peasants.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/maoism">Maoism · Spread &amp; Variants</a></li><li><blockquote>It lay on the Godavari river, with its capital at Potali or Potana, often identified with Bodhan in modern <b>Telangana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/assaka">Assaka</a></li><li><blockquote>The Vemulavada Chalukyas emerged in the 8th century in the Karimnagar region of modern <b>Telangana</b> as feudatories of the Rashtrakuta dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/chalukyas-of-vemulavada">Chalukyas of Vemulavada</a></li><li><blockquote>The town lies in Jogulamba Gadwal district of <b>Telangana</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/gadwal-samasthan">Gadwal Samasthan</a></li></ul></section>
